         "Requires utilization review programs using algorithmic processes to ensure clinical peers make adverse determinations, comply with accreditation standards, and include policies for algorithm verification. Establishes registration every two years with proof of compliance. Sets appeal procedures for adverse determinations.",
         "Amends the Managed Care Reform and Patient Rights Act to require utilization review programs using algorithmic automated processes to ensure that only clinical peers make adverse determinations based on medical necessity.\n\nMandates these programs to register with the Department of Insurance every two years and provide proof of compliance with Health Utilization Management Standards or equivalent standards.\n\nRequires licensed physicians with relevant board certifications to establish criteria for these automated processes and verify their accuracy.\n\nObliges health care plans to have appeals procedures, allowing appeals to be filed orally or in writing, with decisions rendered within specified timeframes.\n\nEnsures confidentiality of patient-specific information obtained during utilization reviews, restricting access to authorized individuals.\n\nPermits adverse determination appeals to an external independent review, with potential further appeals to the Department if found arbitrary or capricious.\n\nAuthorizes the Department to issue corrective actions or cease and desist orders for non-compliance.",
